From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] texture_cache: a GPU-modified join-ignore overlap is replaced, not fatal

JoinImages collects into join_ignore_textures the overlaps it will not join with - a remapped image,
or one the guest maps at the same address and size as the new image - and then deletes them so the
new image owns the address. If such an overlap was written by the GPU since it was made, the loop
calls UNIMPLEMENTED(), which is ASSERT(false && "Unimplemented!") (src/common/assert.h:55): a debug
build aborts the process, taking the session with it.

The port reaches this whenever device memory runs short. CreateImage then falls back to "using an
unbacked sparse image", the next GPU write into that image is "GPU write into unwritten sparse
image ...; replacing it", and that replacement joins over the image it is replacing - which the GPU
had already rendered into. A console log shows 36 aborts in the last six seconds of a run, each
preceded by "Render target in unbacked image" and "PrepareImage: GPU write into unwritten sparse
image ...; replacing it", the first 0.9 s after the driver reported "does not fit; using an unbacked
sparse image" and 1.2 s after the first "is over the memory budget; allocating it anyway". The run
ends on the assertion, not on the missing memory.

Deleting the overlap is what this loop does for every other flag combination, and what this situation
calls for: the new image is at the same address and stands in for it. So log it and fall through to
the same UntrackImage/UnregisterImage/DeleteImage the loop already runs. That matches the choice the
port already made for unbacked images - EDEN_NX2_UNBACKED_LIMIT=0 keeps the session alive with them
instead of ending it - which the abort contradicted.

What this can break: the overlap's contents are dropped without a download, so a surface the game
rendered and then re-created at the same address can show stale or uninitialised texels for a frame
where it was already unbacked. It removes a hard abort; it does not remove the memory exhaustion that
reaches the path, so image degradation under a full device-memory budget is unchanged.

Checked: the patched file differs from the tree the stack produces only in this hunk; applies on the
core plus the 0410 stack. Not run on a console.

diff --git a/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h b/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
--- a/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
+++ b/src/video_core/texture_cache/texture_cache.h
@@ -3394,7 +3394,21 @@
     for (const ImageId overlap_id : join_ignore_textures) {
         Image& overlap = slot_images[overlap_id];
         if (True(overlap.flags & ImageFlagBits::GpuModified)) {
-            UNIMPLEMENTED();
+            // A GPU-modified image the join will not join with is the one the new image replaces: the
+            // same address and size, or a remap, with the GPU having written it since it was made.
+            // Deleting it is what this loop does for every other flag combination, so do it here too
+            // rather than abort - UNIMPLEMENTED() is ASSERT(false && "Unimplemented!")
+            // (common/assert.h), which ends the session in a debug build. Device memory running out
+            // is what reaches this: an image that "does not fit" becomes an unbacked sparse image,
+            // and the next GPU write into it replaces it (a console log shows 36 aborts in the last
+            // six seconds of a run, the first 0.9 s after the driver gave up on the allocation). The
+            // port already keeps the session alive on those images (EDEN_NX2_UNBACKED_LIMIT=0):
+            // losing one frame of an unbacked surface's contents is part of that choice, an abort is
+            // not.
+            static size_t modified_ignored{};
+            LOG_WARNING(HW_GPU,
+                        "Join ignored a GPU-modified image at {:#x}: replacing it ({} so far)",
+                        overlap.gpu_addr, ++modified_ignored);
         }
         if (True(overlap.flags & ImageFlagBits::Tracked)) {
             UntrackImage(overlap, overlap_id);
